                         <td>N/A</td>
                         <td>February 1 to February 25, 2026</td>
                      </tr>
                   </tbody>
                </table>
             </div>

             <div class="section-title">Why Your Response Sheet and Answer Key Matter</div>
             <div class="subsection-paragraph benefit-card">
               <p>✅ <strong>Clarity and Transparency:</strong> The response sheet provides a clear record of your marked answers, ensuring transparency in the evaluation process.</p>
               <p>✅ <strong>Score Estimation:</strong> By comparing your responses with the official answer key, you can accurately calculate your estimated score before the official results are declared.</p>
               <p>✅ <strong>Identify Weak Areas:</strong> Analyzing your performance helps in understanding your mistakes and identifying areas that require further preparation for upcoming sessions or JEE Advanced.</p>
               <p>✅ <strong>Objection Filing:</strong> The response sheet is a crucial document for raising objections against any incorrect answers in the provisional answer key within the specified challenge window.</p>
             </div>

             <div class="section-title">How to Download Your JEE Main 2026 Response Sheet</div>
             <p>Follow these simple steps to access your recorded responses from the NTA JEE Main official website:</p>
             <div class="benefit-item">
               <div class="benefit-title">1. Visit the Official NTA JEE Main Portal</div>
               <div class="benefit-content">Navigate to the official website: jeemain.nta.ac.in or jeemain.nta.nic.in. Ensure you are on the legitimate NTA portal to avoid fraudulent links.</div>
             </div>
             <div class="benefit-item">
               <div class="benefit-title">2. Locate the Download Link</div>
               <div class="benefit-content">On the homepage, look for a prominent link related to "JEE Main 2026 Session 1 Response Sheet &amp; Answer Key" or "View Response Sheet/Recorded Responses".</div>
             </div>
             <div class="benefit-item">
               <div class="benefit-title">3. Log In to Your Dashboard</div>
               <div class="benefit-content">You will be prompted to log in using your JEE Main 2026 application number and either your password or date of birth.</div>
             </div>
             <div class="benefit-item">
               <div class="benefit-title">4. View and Download</div>
               <div class="benefit-content">Once logged in, click on the option to view your Response Sheet. The sheet will open in a new tab. Download and save the PDF for your records. It's advisable to keep a printed copy for future reference, especially for score calculation and raising objections.</div>
             </div>

             <div class="section-title">Understanding the JEE Main 2026 Provisional Answer Key and Challenging Responses</div>
             <p>Alongside your response sheet, the NTA releases a provisional answer key. This key provides the correct answers to all questions asked in the examination. You can use it to cross-verify your responses.</p>
             <div class="benefit-item">
               <div class="benefit-title">1. Compare and Calculate Your Score</div>
               <div class="benefit-content">Match your answers from the response sheet with the correct answers provided in the provisional answer key. Apply the JEE Main marking scheme (+4 for every correct answer, -1 for every incorrect answer) to estimate your raw score.</div>
             </div>
             <div class="benefit-item">
               <div class="benefit-title">2. How to Challenge the Provisional Answer Key</div>
               <div class="benefit-content">If you find any discrepancy or believe an answer in the provisional key is incorrect, NTA allows you to challenge it. The challenge window for Session 1 is open from February 4 to February 5, 2026.</div>
             </div>
             <div class="benefit-item">
               <div class="benefit-title">3. Steps to Raise an Objection</div>
               <div class="benefit-content">
                  <ul>
                     <li>Visit the official JEE Main website and log in.</li>
                     <li>Click on the "Challenge Answer Key" link.</li>
                     <li>Select the question(s) you wish to challenge.</li>
                     <li>Provide supporting proof (e.g., from NCERT textbooks or standard reference books).</li>
                     <li>Pay the non-refundable objection fee of ₹200 per question. Only paid challenges submitted within the stipulated time will be considered.</li>
                     <li>Download the confirmation receipt.</li>
                  </ul>
               </div>
             </div>
             <div class="benefit-item">
               <div class="benefit-title">4. Final Answer Key and Results</div>
               <div class="benefit-content">After reviewing all challenges, NTA will release the final answer key. The JEE Main 2026 Session 1 results, including your scores and probable percentile, will be declared based on this final answer key by February 12, 2026.</div>
             </div>

             <div class="section-title">Looking Ahead: JEE Main 2026 Session 2</div>
             <p>For those who wish to improve their scores or missed the first attempt, registration for JEE Main 2026 Session 2 is currently live and will continue until February 25, 2026. The exams for Session 2 are scheduled to be held from April 2 to April 9, 2026. This provides another excellent opportunity to achieve your desired percentile.</p>

             <p>The release of the JEE Main 2026 Session 1 response sheet and answer key marks a crucial phase for all candidates. Utilize these resources effectively to analyze your performance, estimate your scores, and proceed with confidence towards your academic goals. Good luck!</p>
             </div>
             </div>
